# CONV etcdb - the classic /etc database files
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Tier CONVENTION. Sources: the section 5 manual pages passwd(5), group(5),
# hosts(5), services(5), protocols(5), shells(5) and resolv.conf(5) of the
# three BSD projects (BSD-A, BSD-B, BSD-C as in conv/modules/conv/layout), and
# the IANA protocol numbers registry for CONV.etcdb.014. The pages for the
# other systems' own file format pages were not reachable (that manual host
# is blocked from this build environment); the file formats asserted are those
# the BSD pages share, which are also the classic formats those systems
# inherited. Where the BSD pages differ, the check accepts both.
#
# Every test is NOTINUSE when its file does not exist (another database, or
# none, may be in use). The files are read directly; lines that begin with
# "+" or "-" in passwd and group (compat-mode markers, BSD passwd(5) and
# group(5)) are skipped.
#
# Deliberately not asserted: password field content, the gecos field, the
# existence of a group for every primary gid, login-name character sets
# beyond "no white space, no leading hyphen" (the BSD pages differ on the
# rest), the 1024-byte line limit (BSD pages only), nsswitch order, any file
# that the pages call generated or private (master.passwd, the *.db files).

assertion  CONV.etcdb.001
anchor     passwd(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       Each entry of /etc/passwd has exactly seven colon-separated fields
           and a login name that is non-empty, has no white space and does not
           begin with a hyphen.
test       t/001-passwd-fields.c
note       tier CONVENTION. BSD-A, BSD-B and BSD-C describe master.passwd with
           ten fields and say the passwd file is generated from it with three
           removed, leaving seven. All three forbid a leading hyphen in the
           name; BSD-A forbids white space. Sources agree on the count.
nomutant   reads a file; fixture via PC_CONV_PASSWD (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.etcdb.002
anchor     passwd(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       The uid and gid fields of every /etc/passwd entry are decimal
           integers that fit in 32 bits, no login name is repeated and no uid
           is used by two entries.
test       t/002-passwd-ids.c
note       tier CONVENTION. The three pages say name and uid "should" be unique
           and that duplicates are "usually a mistake", so a duplicate is
           reported for a person (some systems keep a deliberate second
           uid-0 account). Sources agree.
nomutant   reads a file; fixture via PC_CONV_PASSWD (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.etcdb.003
anchor     passwd(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       The home directory field of every /etc/passwd entry is an absolute
           path and the shell field is empty or an absolute path.
test       t/003-passwd-paths.c
note       tier CONVENTION. BSD-A, BSD-B and BSD-C: the home field is a full
           path name; BSD-B and BSD-C: an empty shell field means the default
           shell. Sources agree.
nomutant   reads a file; fixture via PC_CONV_PASSWD (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.etcdb.004
anchor     group(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       Each entry of /etc/group has exactly four colon-separated fields, a
           non-empty name without white space, a decimal integer gid, and a
           member list that is comma-separated with no spaces and no empty
           name; names and gids are not repeated.
test       t/004-group-fields.c
note       tier CONVENTION. The three group(5) pages say four fields, a decimal
           gid and that the member list has commas without spaces; the
           uniqueness is a "should" there too. Sources agree.
nomutant   reads a file; fixture via PC_CONV_GROUP (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.etcdb.005
anchor     passwd(5)#DESCRIPTION
anchor     group(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       For up to 300 entries of each file, getpwnam() and getgrnam() find
           the entry and return the uid, gid, home, shell (when the file gives
           one) and group members that the file lists.
test       t/005-db-agree.c
note       tier CONVENTION. The pages define the files as the databases that
           getpwent(3) and getgrent(3) read. NOTINUSE if the files are
           overridden. A system whose lookup order puts another database ahead
           of the files may legitimately differ: a person judges.
nomutant   compares library results with the files; no library fault planted

assertion  CONV.etcdb.006
anchor     shells(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       Each non-comment line of /etc/shells is one absolute path to an
           executable regular file.
test       t/006-shells-format.c
note       tier CONVENTION. All three pages: the file lists the shells on the
           system, one path relative to the root per line, comments after a
           hash mark. BSD-C adds "valid shells available". Sources agree.
nomutant   reads a file; fixture via PC_CONV_SHELLS (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.etcdb.007
anchor     shells(5)#DESCRIPTION
anchor     passwd(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       The shell field of each uid-0 account that names one is listed in
           /etc/shells.
test       t/007-root-shell-listed.c
note       tier CONVENTION, practice. The pages say programs consult the shells
           file to decide whether an account's shell is valid; they do not say
           the superuser's must be listed. Sources silent on the exact point.
nomutant   reads two files; fixtures via PC_CONV_PASSWD and PC_CONV_SHELLS (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.etcdb.008
anchor     hosts(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       Each non-comment line of /etc/hosts is an IPv4 or IPv6 address
           followed by one or more host names of printable characters.
test       t/008-hosts-format.c
note       tier CONVENTION. The three hosts(5) pages: address, official name,
           aliases, separated by blanks or tabs; hash starts a comment.
           Sources agree.
nomutant   reads a file; fixture via PC_CONV_HOSTS (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.etcdb.009
anchor     hosts(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       /etc/hosts contains a line that gives the name localhost a loopback
           address (127.x.y.z or ::1).
test       t/009-hosts-loopback.c
note       tier CONVENTION, practice. Only BSD-C says localhost always
           resolves to the loopback addresses; none says the file must list
           it (a resolver may supply it). Reported for a person; see also
           CONV.etcdb.010, which asserts the resolution itself.
nomutant   reads a file; fixture via PC_CONV_HOSTS (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.etcdb.010
anchor     hosts(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       getaddrinfo() for the name localhost returns at least one address,
           every address is a loopback address, and when the hosts file lists
           an address for localhost one of them is returned.
test       t/010-resolve-localhost.c
note       tier CONVENTION. Only BSD-C states that localhost always resolves
           to a loopback address (the other two pages are silent); the file
           agreement follows from the pages calling the file the host
           database. The interface is POSIX getaddrinfo(); the obsolescent
           gethostbyname() is not used. NOTINUSE if the file is overridden.
nomutant   compares library results with the file; no library fault planted

assertion  CONV.etcdb.011
anchor     services(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       Each non-comment line of /etc/services has a name, a port/protocol
           item with a port number up to 65535 and a lower-case protocol name,
           then optional aliases.
test       t/011-services-format.c
note       tier CONVENTION. The three services(5) pages: official name, port
           and protocol joined by a slash, aliases. Sources agree.
nomutant   reads a file; fixture via PC_CONV_SERVICES (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.etcdb.012
anchor     services(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       For up to 400 tcp or udp entries whose official name is used by no
           other entry (as name or alias) for the same protocol,
           getservbyname() returns the port the file lists.
test       t/012-services-agree.c
note       tier CONVENTION. The pages define the file as the database
           getservent(3) reads. NOTINUSE if overridden. Sources agree.
nomutant   compares library results with the file; no library fault planted

assertion  CONV.etcdb.013
anchor     protocols(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       Each non-comment line of /etc/protocols has a name, a protocol
           number up to 255 (the IP header's protocol field is eight bits),
           then optional aliases.
test       t/013-protocols-format.c
note       tier CONVENTION. The three protocols(5) pages: official name,
           number, aliases. The 255 bound is the width of the IP header field
           that the numbers fill (IANA registry range 0 to 255). Sources agree.
nomutant   reads a file; fixture via PC_CONV_PROTOCOLS (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.etcdb.014
anchor     protocol-numbers.xml#icmp
anchor     protocols(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       The protocols file has entries making icmp 1, tcp 6 and udp 17, and
           getprotobyname() gives the same numbers.
test       t/014-protocol-numbers.c
note       tier CONVENTION; the numbers themselves are FORMAL (IANA protocol
           numbers registry, which RFC 768 and RFC 9293 rely on), the file
           and its content are convention. Kept under disputed because the
           requirement that the file carry all three is the pages' "known
           protocols" wording only. getprotobyname() is compared only when the
           real file is used.
nomutant   reads a file; fixture via PC_CONV_PROTOCOLS (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.etcdb.015
anchor     resolv.conf(5)#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
says       Each non-comment line of /etc/resolv.conf starts with a documented
           directive (nameserver, domain, search, sortlist, options, lookup,
           family) followed by a value; a nameserver value is an IP address and
           domain takes one name.
test       t/015-resolv-conf.c
note       tier CONVENTION. Directive set is the union over the three
           resolv.conf(5) pages (BSD-C documents lookup and family, BSD-B calls
           lookup ignored, BSD-A does not list it); comments start with hash or semicolon
           (BSD-C). Sources disagree on the set, so every directive documented
           by any is accepted.
nomutant   reads a file; fixture via PC_CONV_RESOLV (conv/neg/run.sh)
